{
  "walkthrough": [
    "Goal: week 2's perspective (V大's 透视盒子, with Drawabox's checks): complete a box in perspective from its front corner, draw ellipses that fit their frames, and draw boxes freely on a horizon; then see the geometry itself, the exact answer over my lines, and judge my copy against it by eye: nothing of my strokes is read (S145).",
    "Entry: a lesson of Boxes at level 1 from the home: two boxes to complete (a two-point box, then a three-point one), the first a tall box across the horizon. Then Home, a lesson of Ellipses at level 1 (four ellipses in frames), Home, a lesson of Free boxes at level 1 (one page).",
    "The box: in the left frame the horizon (labelled) and the box's front corner with its three edges (of a box across the horizon, the corner farther from it); the same in the empty frame beside it, where I draw. The first time, the instructions show by themselves: near is bigger, far is smaller; edges running the same way meet at one vanishing point, those parallel to the ground on the horizon; judge where the given edges meet (the points may lie off the frame); draw the other nine edges with Draw, the hidden ones too, each in one sure stroke, ghosted first; Sketch for construction lines; then Compare. The tools beside the frame: Sketch, Draw, Undo, Redo.",
    "Copy: a construction line with Sketch, then the nine edges with Draw, one of the bottom edges a little off.",
    "Compare: the exact box lies over mine in red, its hidden edges dashed, its two vanishing points ringed red off the frame; my edges are not read or drawn on, and no line says what is off: the edge I drew off stands apart from the red. The card: Construction, How close is it?, Accurate, Almost, Again. No In place or Aligned: geometry has one answer. The pen is blue: I redraw the edge over the wrong one; the overlay goes as my pen lands; Compare again: the red over my correction. I choose Accurate, kept with the exercise once.",
    "The ellipse: a plain frame (a table's cell) in my one frame. Its instructions: draw the ellipse that touches the middle of each side; in perspective the middles lie nearer the far ends, found through the diagonals; one stroke through the middles from the shoulder; its paragraph says only how to practise. I draw it a little narrow and to the left. Compare: the exact ellipse red over mine, nothing ringed, no words; the card How close is it?.",
    "Chinese: All tools, Actions, 中文: the card reads 椭圆, 看画法, 准, 差一点, 重来. I draw the ellipse again over it in blue; Compare lays the exact one over both; I choose 差一点 (Almost). Home leaves the lesson under way.",
    "Free boxes: a lesson of Free boxes: a page on a middle horizon (视平线). Its instructions: three or four boxes freely, each turned another way and of other proportions; verticals vertical; each box's edges of a direction meet at one point on the horizon; front corner wider than 90°, about 120. I draw three boxes, every edge and the hidden ones: one well, one whose vanishing points sit too close (its bottom corner narrow), one with an edge turned off.",
    "Compare: the page's horizon run on across the sheet, faint; nothing of my boxes is read, ringed or marked: the narrow corner and the turned edge are mine to see, as the How says, by running my edges on in pencil. The card: How close is it?, Accurate, Almost, Again. Back in English, the same in English; I choose Accurate; Finish shows it under the page.",
    "Not here: single shapes (s80); boxes and ellipses on the tablet's own hand (the author's)."
  ],
